.Dd April 28, 2026
.Dt NEXTUFS 1
.Os Linux
.Sh NAME
.Nm nextufs
.Nd inspect, mount, check, repair, create, resize, and modify NeXT UFS images
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs [-h|--help]
nextufs --version
nextufs info [--json] <source>
nextufs browse <source> [path]
nextufs mount <source> <mountpoint> [fuse options ...]
nextufs fsck [-n|-N|-y|-Y] [-p] [-P] [-b block] [-d] <source> [...]
nextufs mkimg [options] <target> <size> [raw-geometry-args...]
nextufs resize grow [--force-size] <source> <size-1k-sectors>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] <source> <path> <contents>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] <operation> <source> <operation-args...>
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a Linux userspace tool for working with
.Tn NEXTSTEP
and
.Tn OPENSTEP
UFS filesystems.
It uses a single public command with subcommands for source inspection,
filesystem browsing, FUSE mounting, metadata checking and repair, image
creation, offline growth, and offline file mutation.
.Pp
.Nm
understands raw UFS filesystem images, labeled NeXT disk images, standalone
VirtualBox VDI images, and VirtualBox differencing VDI chains.
For VDI chains, pass the current chain head to
.Nm .
Use
.Pa tools/vdi_chain.py
from the
.Pa tools/
directory to identify the base, intermediate, and head images in a VM
directory.
.Sh GLOBAL OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Show top-level usage.
.It Fl -version
Show the
.Nm
version.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Ss info
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs info [--json] <source>
.Ed
.Pp
Show source layout and filesystem information.
The report includes source type, source size, slice layout, filesystem size,
compatibility ceiling, block and fragment geometry, cylinder group counts,
free-space counters, and disk-label details when present.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -json
Emit JSON instead of human-readable text.
The JSON output is built from the same collected source and filesystem
information as the text report.
.El
.Ss browse
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs browse <source> [path]
.Ed
.Pp
Browse a filesystem without mounting it.
With no
.Ar path ,
prints root inode information and the root directory listing.
With
.Ar path ,
also resolves that path and prints inode information.
Directory targets are listed, symbolic links show their target, and regular
files include a short data preview.
.Ss mount
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs mount <source> <mountpoint> [fuse options]
.Ed
.Pp
Mount a source image through FUSE.
Mounts default to read-only unless a writable mount is explicitly requested.
Options not handled by
.Nm
are passed through to FUSE.
.Pp
The following
.Fl o
options are interpreted by
.Nm :
.Bl -tag -width "mode=user"
.It Cm ro
Open the source read-only.
This is the default when neither
.Cm ro
nor
.Cm rw
is supplied.
.It Cm rw
Open the source read-write and allow mutating FUSE operations.
.It Cm mode=su
Use superuser-style filesystem access policy.
In read-only mounts, this allows full read access through the mounted
filesystem.
In writable mounts, it allows write operations without enforcing image-owner
permission checks.
This is the default mount policy.
.It Cm mode=user
Use user-style filesystem access policy.
Permission checks are enforced using the caller's FUSE uid/gid, unless
overridden by
.Cm uid=
or
.Cm gid= .
.It Cm uid Ns = Ns Ar uid
Override the uid used for permission checks in
.Cm mode=user .
.It Cm gid Ns = Ns Ar gid
Override the gid used for permission checks in
.Cm mode=user .
.El
.Pp
Examples:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs mount image.img /mnt/nextufs -f -s
nextufs mount image.img /mnt/nextufs -o rw,mode=su -f -s
nextufs mount image.img /mnt/nextufs -o mode=user,uid=1000,gid=100 -f -s
.Ed
.Ss fsck
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs fsck [-n|-y] [-p] [-P] [-b block] [-d] <source> [...]
.Ed
.Pp
Check and optionally repair UFS metadata.
.Nm
.Cm fsck
uses the shared source backend, so the same raw, labeled, standalone VDI, and
VDI-chain source handling is used by the rest of the tool.
.Pp
Pass the current VDI chain head when checking or repairing a VDI chain.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n , Fl N
Answer no to repair prompts.
This is the usual non-mutating check mode.
.It Fl y , Fl Y
Answer yes to repair prompts.
This repairs metadata and modifies the source.
.It Fl p
Run in preen mode.
.It Fl P
Force preen mode.
.It Fl b Ar block
Use an alternate superblock location.
The block may be supplied as
.Fl b Ns Ar block
or
.Fl b Ar block .
.It Fl d
Enable checker debug output.
.El
.Ss mkimg
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs mkimg [options] <target> <size> [raw-geometry-args...]
.Ed
.Pp
Create a new UFS image.
By default,
.Nm
.Cm mkimg
creates a labeled NeXT disk image with a 160 KiB front porch, NeXT disk label,
and UFS root slice.
With
.Fl -raw ,
it creates a raw UFS filesystem starting at byte zero.
.Pp
Existing targets are not overwritten unless
.Fl -force-overwrite
is supplied.
.Pp
Bare numeric sizes are interpreted differently by mode:
.Bl -bullet
.It
For labeled images, a bare number is bytes.
.It
For raw images, a bare number is 1 KiB sectors.
.El
.Pp
Sizes may also use
.Cm K ,
.Cm M ,
or
.Cm G
suffixes.
Suffixes always represent bytes.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -raw
Create a raw UFS filesystem image at byte zero.
.It Fl -label Ar name
Set the NeXT disk label name for a labeled image.
This option is invalid with
.Fl -raw .
If omitted, the label is derived from the target filename.
.It Fl -dry-run
Print the planned layout without creating or modifying the target.
.It Fl -force-size
Allow a target size above the NEXTSTEP/OPENSTEP compatibility ceiling.
Without this option,
.Nm
rejects sizes above 4294836224 bytes, or 4194176 1 KiB sectors.
.It Fl -force-overwrite
Replace an existing target file.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Show
.Cm mkimg
usage.
.El
.Pp
When
.Fl -raw
is used, optional raw geometry arguments may follow
.Ar size :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nsect ntrak bsize fsize cpg minfree rps nbpi opt
.Ed
.Pp
These are low-level UFS formatter parameters:
sectors per track, tracks per cylinder, block size, fragment size, cylinders
per group, minimum free percentage, revolutions per second, bytes per inode,
and optimization preference.
They are intended for reproducibility and compatibility testing; ordinary use
should omit them.
.Ss resize grow
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs resize grow [--force-size] <source> <size-1k-sectors>
.Ed
.Pp
Grow a supported raw UFS image or supported single-slice labeled NeXT disk
image offline.
For labeled disk images, the requested size is the final image size.
For raw filesystem images, the requested size is the final filesystem size.
.Pp
Bare numeric sizes are interpreted as 1 KiB sectors.
Sizes may also use
.Cm K ,
.Cm M ,
or
.Cm G
suffixes, which represent bytes.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -force-size
Allow a target size above the NEXTSTEP/OPENSTEP compatibility ceiling.
.El
.Ss mkfile
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] <source> <path> <contents>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --unlink <source> <path>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --mkdir <source> <path>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --overwrite <source> <path> <contents>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --append <source> <path> <contents>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --rmdir <source> <path>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --link <source> <source-path> <target-path>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --rename <source> <source-path> <target-path>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --symlink <source> <target> <link-path>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --truncate <source> <path> <size>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --pwrite <source> <path> <offset> <contents>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --mknod <source> <path> <octal-mode> <rdev>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --chmod <source> <path> <octal-mode>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --chown <source> <path> <uid|keep> <gid|keep>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --utimes <source> <path> <atime> <mtime>
nextufs mkfile [--policy su|user] [--uid uid] [--gid gid] --from-file <source> <path> <host-file>
.Ed
.Pp
Apply offline file and directory mutations directly to a source image.
This command is useful for testing, recovery work, and scripted image changes.
It modifies the source.
.Pp
Common
.Cm mkfile
options:
.Bl -tag -width "--policy su"
.It Fl -policy Cm su
Use superuser-style mutation policy.
This is the default.
.It Fl -policy Cm user
Use user-style mutation policy and enforce ownership and permission checks.
If
.Fl -uid
or
.Fl -gid
is omitted, that id remains 0.
.It Fl -uid Ar uid
Set the uid used by
.Cm --policy user .
.It Fl -gid Ar gid
Set the gid used by
.Cm --policy user .
.El
.Pp
Operations:
.Bl -tag -width "--overwrite"
.It Ar source path contents
Create a regular file at
.Ar path
with string
.Ar contents .
Fails if the path already exists.
.It Fl -unlink Ar source path
Remove a non-directory entry.
.It Fl -mkdir Ar source path
Create a directory.
.It Fl -overwrite Ar source path contents
Replace the contents of an existing regular file with
.Ar contents .
.It Fl -append Ar source path contents
Append
.Ar contents
to an existing regular file.
.It Fl -rmdir Ar source path
Remove an empty directory.
.It Fl -link Ar source source-path target-path
Create a hard link from
.Ar target-path
to
.Ar source-path .
.It Fl -rename Ar source source-path target-path
Rename or move
.Ar source-path
to
.Ar target-path .
.It Fl -symlink Ar source target link-path
Create a symbolic link at
.Ar link-path
whose target string is
.Ar target .
.It Fl -truncate Ar source path size
Resize an existing regular file to
.Ar size
bytes.
.It Fl -pwrite Ar source path offset contents
Write
.Ar contents
to an existing regular file starting at byte
.Ar offset .
.It Fl -mknod Ar source path octal-mode rdev
Create a special node with mode
.Ar octal-mode
and device value
.Ar rdev .
.It Fl -chmod Ar source path octal-mode
Change the mode bits of
.Ar path .
.It Fl -chown Ar source path uid|keep gid|keep
Change owner and group.
Use
.Cm keep
or
.Cm -1
for either field to leave it unchanged.
.It Fl -utimes Ar source path atime mtime
Set access and modification timestamps.
Times are integer Unix timestamps.
.It Fl -from-file Ar source path host-file
Create a regular file at
.Ar path
using bytes read from
.Ar host-file .
.El
.Sh SOURCE TYPES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Raw UFS filesystem image
The UFS filesystem begins at byte zero.
.It Labeled NeXT disk image
The source contains a NeXT disk label and a UFS slice.
.It Standalone VDI
A supported VirtualBox VDI image.
.It VDI chain
A VirtualBox differencing chain.
Pass the current head image.
.Nm
resolves parent images by UUID through the shared VDI source backend.
.El
.Sh SIZE VALUES
Size arguments accept positive integers.
The suffixes
.Cm K ,
.Cm M ,
and
.Cm G
mean KiB, MiB, and GiB byte quantities.
.Pp
The meaning of a bare number is command-specific:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Cm mkimg
labeled mode: bytes.
.It
.Cm mkimg --raw :
1 KiB sectors.
.It
.Cm resize grow :
1 KiB sectors.
.El
.Pp
The default NEXTSTEP/OPENSTEP compatibility ceiling is 4294836224 bytes,
or 4194176 1 KiB sectors.
Commands that create or grow images refuse larger sizes unless
.Fl -force-size
is supplied.
.Sh SAFETY
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Cm info ,
.Cm browse ,
and
.Cm fsck -n
are intended to be non-mutating.
.It
.Cm fsck -y ,
writable FUSE mounts,
.Cm resize grow ,
and
.Cm mkfile
modify the source.
.It
Work on a copy before repairing, resizing, or mutating an image that matters.
.It
For VDI chains, writes go through the chain head.
Passing a base image or older snapshot is not equivalent to modifying the
current VM state.
.It
.Cm mkimg
refuses to overwrite existing targets unless
.Fl -force-overwrite
is supplied.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Show source and filesystem information:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs info disk.img
nextufs info --json disk.img
.Ed
.Pp
Browse a file without mounting:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs browse disk.img /etc/passwd
.Ed
.Pp
Mount read-only:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mkdir -p /tmp/nextufs-mnt
nextufs mount disk.img /tmp/nextufs-mnt -f -s
.Ed
.Pp
Mount read-write with superuser-style policy:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs mount disk.img /tmp/nextufs-mnt -o rw,mode=su -f -s
.Ed
.Pp
Check without modifying:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs fsck -n disk.img
.Ed
.Pp
Repair a copied image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cp disk.img disk-repair.img
nextufs fsck -y disk-repair.img
.Ed
.Pp
Create a labeled 256 MiB NeXT disk image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs mkimg nextufs.img 256M
.Ed
.Pp
Create a raw UFS image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs mkimg --raw nextufs.raw 256M
.Ed
.Pp
Create a file offline:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nextufs mkfile disk.img /private/tmp/example "hello"
.Ed
.Pp
Trace a VDI chain:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tools/vdi_chain.py trace "/path/to/VM/Snapshots/{uuid}.vdi"
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Pp
Command usage errors generally return 2.
Runtime failures generally return 1.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "tools/vdi_chain.py"
.It Pa tools/vdi_chain.py
Utility for scanning and tracing VirtualBox VDI parent/child chains.
.It Pa tests/fsck/
Reproducible fsck corruption fixtures and repair tests.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fsck 8 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr fusermount3 1
.Sh BUGS
Shrinking filesystems is not implemented.
.Pp
.Cm resize grow
supports raw images and supported single-slice labeled disk images.
.Pp
Some low-level
.Cm mkimg --raw
geometry arguments are exposed for reproducibility and compatibility testing
rather than normal day-to-day image creation.
